Style/WordArray byte safety / Issue #172
RuboCop reference: 1.89.0. Four constructed literal inputs, no corpus execution.
Container: UTF-8 / LF; SHA256 e607715b177dda3985b812bce5216b7d8ff2ef068a9598d4cd9f8e9e1fa978cc
Pinned diagnostic API stdout SHA256 20a2cd8c519dd538cee6475b1c8439c756ea312b3f05d00795f523aa0ec317da

The original unsafe source is 107 ASCII bytes with a UTF-8 declaration.
TargetRubyVersion2.7/parser_whitequark is the diagnostic setting. Value records
use MRI4.0.7 and do not prove execution on a Ruby2.7 VM.

One original LF input was inspected without correction. Before inspection the
API asserts parser3.3.12.0, rubocop-ast1.50.0, Prism1.8.1, JSON2.18.0,
RuboCop1.89.0 and display_cop_names:false. Message hex/encoding/validity are
kept separately from the scrubbed UTF-8 explanation. Input/config bytes, size,
mtime and mode were unchanged. The first capture with parser3.3.13.0 and the
cop-name prefix was not adopted. No standard JSON success is inferred.

The earlier real CLI records retain inspect/-a/-A correction bytes and errors.
Their exact dependency selection beyond RuboCop1.89.0 and MRI4.0.7 was not
recorded: the later API pins are not assigned to the earlier records. The unsafe
CLI writes raw0x95, MRI rejects it, and JSON fails with exit2. These failures
remain failures. The common 81-byte notice is preserved; the three safe-control
reports are formatter segments after the exact notice, not whole-stdout JSON.

Historical native U+FFFD output is evidence, never the safe expectation.
Independent Codex policy review supports file-wide rollback to original bytes,
uncorrected diagnostics, a value-byte reason and exit2. No Claude run is claimed.
Invalid reference message bytes are not declared equal to native UTF-8 display.
Candidate LF/CRLF/cache/settings/directive/stdin checks test this safety policy;
reference correction bytes were measured only for LF.

Use a new temporary directory with the stored source/config. Remove ENV4
(RUBYOPT/RUBYLIB/RUBOCOP_OPTS/RUBOCOP_TARGET_RUBY_VERSION) in a child copy and
preserve parent/GEM/PATH/SystemRoot. The single-input diagnostic API was:

```ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true
# 既存1 literalを検査するだけで、補正やJSON formatterのfinishedは呼ばない。
PINS = { 'parser' => '3.3.12.0', 'rubocop-ast' => '1.50.0', 'prism' => '1.8.1', 'json' => '2.18.0', 'rubocop' => '1.89.0' }.freeze
PINS.each { |name, version| gem name, "= #{version}" }
require 'rubocop'
require 'json'
require 'stringio'
raise 'gem pins differ' unless PINS.all? { |name, version| Gem.loaded_specs.fetch(name).version.to_s == version }
config = RuboCop::ConfigLoader.configuration_from_file(File.expand_path('config.yml'))
source = File.binread('input.rb')
processed = RuboCop::ProcessedSource.new(source, 2.7, 'input.rb', parser_engine: :parser_whitequark)
options = { autocorrect: false, raise_error: true, display_cop_names: false }
raise 'cop display option differs' unless options.fetch(:display_cop_names) == false
team = RuboCop::Cop::Team.mobilize([RuboCop::Cop::Style::WordArray], config, options)
report = team.investigate(processed)
raise 'unexpected inspection error' unless team.errors.empty?
formatter = RuboCop::Formatter::JSONFormatter.new(StringIO.new)
fields = report.offenses.map do |offense|
  value = formatter.hash_for_offense(offense)
  message = value.delete(:message)
  value[:message_record] = { hex: message.b.unpack1('H*'), encoding: message.encoding.name,
                             valid_encoding: message.valid_encoding?,
                             scrubbed_UTF8_display_not_raw: message.dup.force_encoding(Encoding::UTF_8).scrub }
  value
end
STDOUT.write(JSON.generate(rubocop_version: RuboCop::Version::STRING, ruby_version: RUBY_VERSION,
                          parser_version: Parser::VERSION, target_ruby: 2.7,
                          parser_engine: 'parser_whitequark', autocorrect: false, display_cop_names: false,
                          actual_gem_versions: PINS.to_h { |name, _| [name, Gem.loaded_specs.fetch(name).version.to_s] },
                          input_valid_syntax: processed.valid_syntax?, errors: team.errors.length,
                          offenses: fields))
```

